<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mesalim of Kish</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2600 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/27th-century-bce">27th century BCE</a> · Also known as Mesilim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mesalim of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> was an Early Dynastic king of Kish who reigned around 2600 BCE and claimed authority over other Sumerian cities. Inscriptions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> record that he arbitrated the border dispute between Lagash and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> over the Gu-edena plain and set up a boundary stone. His settlement did not hold, and the quarrel between the two cities lasted for generations.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…is known of the first two rulers: En-hegal, believed to be the first, appears on a tablet recording a purchase of land, and his status and date are disputed; Lugalshaengur is named as ensi of Lagash on a mace head of <b>Mesilim</b>, who called himself king of Kish.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kings exercised influence far beyond the city: <b>Mesalim of Kish</b> acted as arbiter in the border dispute between Lagash and Umma, and later rulers of other cities, including Eannatum of Lagash, adopted the title 'king of Kish' to claim wider authority.</blockquote><a href="/state/kish">Kish</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers clashed with Lagash over the fertile Gu-edena plain, a dispute that <b>Mesalim of Kish</b> tried to settle by setting up a boundary stone around 2600 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/umma">Umma</a></li></ul></section>
